WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.517 --> 00:00:04.060
- And I think people skills
tend to be highly underestimated

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.424
in terms of just ability to run a company.

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:09.735
Guys, I'm going to major
in computer science

00:00:09.735 --> 00:00:11.458
and one of my friend said, wow,

00:00:11.458 --> 00:00:13.231
that's the stupidest
thing I've ever heard.

00:00:13.231 --> 00:00:14.581
And like they come at me again,

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:16.723
you anonymous quote me?
(audience laughing)

00:00:16.723 --> 00:00:18.179
- I can't wait to get an anonymous quote--

00:00:18.179 --> 00:00:19.130
- (chuckles) I kill you!

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.145
Our goal is to be the most
important venture capital firm

00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:23.224
in the world, following your passion

00:00:23.224 --> 00:00:25.379
is a very me-centered view of the world.

00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:28.845
Work is what's important, it's that effort

00:00:28.845 --> 00:00:30.900
where you learn the secrets.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.407
I wanted to quit every day
(chuckles) I was in that job.

00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:35.303
I felt like throwing up when
I woke up in the morning.

00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:38.327
But you only get leverage if the person

00:00:38.327 --> 00:00:41.993
that you hire can do
it better than you can.

00:00:41.993 --> 00:00:44.205
If you're a technology
entrepreneur, you've got

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:46.940
every opportunity to make
like a gazillion dollars.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.656
Like all the VCs and everybody tells me,

00:00:49.656 --> 00:00:53.608
Ben, you know what the
key to a great company is?

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:55.539
Hire A players.

00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:58.434
- He's an American
businessman, investor, blogger

00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:01.169
and author, he's the co-founder
of the venture capital firm

00:01:01.169 --> 00:01:02.795
that bears his man, he co-founded

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:05.196
the enterprise software company Opsware

00:01:05.196 --> 00:01:08.875
that Hewlett Packard
acquired for $1.6 billion.

00:01:08.875 --> 00:01:10.782
He's Ben Horowitz and here are

00:01:10.782 --> 00:01:13.274
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:13.274 --> 00:01:15.640
(zapping)
(thunder rumbling)

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.515
- Yeah, so in the world of
entrepreneurship, the big skills

00:01:20.515 --> 00:01:24.485
are programming skills and people skills,

00:01:24.485 --> 00:01:26.506
like (chuckles) those are
the big two and I think

00:01:26.506 --> 00:01:29.562
people skills tend to
be highly underestimated

00:01:29.562 --> 00:01:33.739
in terms of just ability to
run a company and so forth.

00:01:33.739 --> 00:01:37.644
And what I mean by people
skills is the ability

00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:41.603
to understand other people's motivation

00:01:41.603 --> 00:01:43.873
and other people's motivations
who you're talking to

00:01:43.873 --> 00:01:46.322
and then who you're also
not talking to and how

00:01:46.322 --> 00:01:48.523
they're going to think
about something and where

00:01:48.523 --> 00:01:52.074
they're coming from and
relate to that in a way

00:01:52.074 --> 00:01:54.798
that gets you to the right
conclusion about you build

00:01:54.798 --> 00:01:58.415
an organization or build
a company or make a deal

00:01:58.415 --> 00:02:01.917
or any of these kinds of
things as incredibly valuable

00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:06.084
and that's what I would say
most of the good entrepreneurs

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.236
end up lacking more than the
technical skills that we see.

00:02:11.236 --> 00:02:14.792
Now being like a boss-level
engineer, that's extremely

00:02:14.792 --> 00:02:18.205
valuable, I'm not
discounting that but that's

00:02:18.205 --> 00:02:21.025
really the other big, big
skill that you can learn.

00:02:21.025 --> 00:02:23.733
And I was just so excited
to tell my friends.

00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:26.053
I as like, man, they're going
to be just so fired for me.

00:02:26.053 --> 00:02:27.935
I figured it out, I'm not
going to be stressed anymore.

00:02:27.935 --> 00:02:30.828
I'm like, guys, I'm going
to major in computer science

00:02:30.828 --> 00:02:32.596
and one of my friends said, wow,

00:02:32.596 --> 00:02:34.588
that's the stupidest
thing I've ever heard.

00:02:34.588 --> 00:02:36.372
(audience laughing)

00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:40.291
And I'm like, why, he's like,
you're at Columbia University.

00:02:40.291 --> 00:02:43.744
That's like a trade, you
could learn that at DeVry.

00:02:43.744 --> 00:02:45.890
They'll teach you how to build computers,

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.105
fix them, program them.
(audience chuckles)

00:02:49.105 --> 00:02:52.001
You should major in something real.

00:02:52.001 --> 00:02:53.676
And I was just thinking to myself.

00:02:53.676 --> 00:02:55.588
I was like I'm talking
about a limitless machine.

00:02:55.588 --> 00:02:57.438
You're talking about a washing machine.

00:02:57.438 --> 00:02:59.564
(audience laughing)

00:02:59.564 --> 00:03:02.084
But I was so frustrated, I
couldn't even really explain

00:03:02.084 --> 00:03:05.109
to him why but it was at that point,

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:06.880
at my height of frustration that I learned

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.727
the most valuable lesson
that I learned at Columbia,

00:03:09.727 --> 00:03:12.570
which is don't listen to your friends.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:15.570
(audience laughing)

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:21.512
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:03:21.512 --> 00:03:25.892
And the more generalized
case of don't listen

00:03:25.892 --> 00:03:29.070
to your friends is think for yourself.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.573
And thinking for yourself
sounds both simple

00:03:31.573 --> 00:03:35.088
and trivial but in reality,
it's extremely difficult

00:03:35.088 --> 00:03:37.863
and it's profound and here's why.

00:03:37.863 --> 00:03:41.657
As human beings, we want to be
liked, it's anthropological.

00:03:41.657 --> 00:03:43.687
If people didn't like you in caveman days,

00:03:43.687 --> 00:03:46.535
they would just eat you.
(audience chuckles)

00:03:46.535 --> 00:03:49.153
So you really have a
natural built-in instinct

00:03:49.153 --> 00:03:51.041
to want to be liked and
the easiest way to be liked

00:03:51.041 --> 00:03:53.445
is to tell people what they want to hear.

00:03:53.445 --> 00:03:55.717
And you know what everybody wants to hear?

00:03:55.717 --> 00:03:58.258
What they already believe to be true

00:03:58.258 --> 00:04:01.470
and so the last thing they want
to hear is an original idea

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.845
that contradicts their belief system

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:08.120
so it's very hard to bring
that kind of stuff up

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.556
but those are the things,
those are the only things,

00:04:11.556 --> 00:04:14.829
things that you believe,
that everybody around you

00:04:14.829 --> 00:04:17.723
doesn't believe, when you're right,

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:20.639
that create real value in the world.

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:22.313
Everything else, people already know.

00:04:22.313 --> 00:04:25.847
There's no value created,
it's just business as usual.

00:04:25.847 --> 00:04:28.807
So it's so important
to think for yourself.

00:04:28.807 --> 00:04:31.974
- How do you get along with other VCs?

00:04:32.894 --> 00:04:36.745
- So (chuckles) this is funny
so (chuckles) I get along

00:04:36.745 --> 00:04:39.147
with them very well so there are VCs

00:04:39.147 --> 00:04:42.954
that I really, really like,
Neil Bu-zree is one of them

00:04:42.954 --> 00:04:45.743
who I work with all the
time on various deals.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:48.215
I just got off the phone with Ted Schlein,

00:04:48.215 --> 00:04:50.533
who I get along with very, very well

00:04:50.533 --> 00:04:52.732
so there are a lot of
VCs I get along with.

00:04:52.732 --> 00:04:57.589
I'm a bad cultural fit for
investing, you may have noticed.

00:04:57.589 --> 00:04:59.462
And then there's a good reason for this

00:04:59.462 --> 00:05:03.462
so if you think about the
investing environment,

00:05:05.481 --> 00:05:07.892
it's this weird coopetition
thing because you compete

00:05:07.892 --> 00:05:09.953
for deals but you work on deals together

00:05:09.953 --> 00:05:12.637
so you're kind of partners
but you're competitors

00:05:12.637 --> 00:05:15.152
and I don't come from that culture.

00:05:15.152 --> 00:05:17.508
I come from enterprise software, right?

00:05:17.508 --> 00:05:19.429
Like you know who the top dog
in enterprise software is,

00:05:19.429 --> 00:05:23.025
Larry Ellison, right,
like ain't no co-petition

00:05:23.025 --> 00:05:25.862
in Larry Ellison, right?
(Sarah and audience chuckling)

00:05:25.862 --> 00:05:28.781
All you have to do is
mention anybody's who's close

00:05:28.781 --> 00:05:31.451
to a competitor and his head will blow off

00:05:31.451 --> 00:05:33.282
and he'll go bananas--
- Speaking of a deal,

00:05:33.282 --> 00:05:35.191
we saw that at D with Workday.

00:05:35.191 --> 00:05:36.438
- Yeah, he just went absolutely banana.

00:05:36.438 --> 00:05:38.236
It's like it's on, baby!
(Sarah laughs)

00:05:38.236 --> 00:05:41.228
It's like he stepped on my shoes.

00:05:41.228 --> 00:05:44.478
Let's go, and so for me, and then also,

00:05:46.069 --> 00:05:48.618
I listen to Little Weezy.

00:05:48.618 --> 00:05:51.319
(Sarah and audience laughing)

00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:54.343
And so sometimes, the venture
capitalists with come at me

00:05:54.343 --> 00:05:57.037
being friendly, hey, with the peace sign.

00:05:57.037 --> 00:05:59.195
And to me, the peace
sign is just the trigger

00:05:59.195 --> 00:06:00.900
and the middle finger, you know?

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.733
(Sarah and audience laughing)

00:06:06.978 --> 00:06:08.978
- So that's what I mean.

00:06:11.086 --> 00:06:12.669
That is from Weezy.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.758
- (chuckles) Does it bother you

00:06:15.758 --> 00:06:17.443
when they talk shit about you?

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:18.536
'Cause they don't do it to your face.

00:06:18.536 --> 00:06:19.732
They do it to people like me.

00:06:19.732 --> 00:06:22.817
- It's anonymous, so yeah, that was,

00:06:22.817 --> 00:06:24.494
you're alluding a little
bit to when I called them

00:06:24.494 --> 00:06:28.689
cry babies, which gets
actually another thing.

00:06:28.689 --> 00:06:30.461
The one thing that does
bother me a little bit

00:06:30.461 --> 00:06:33.473
about some VCs and some
angels, so one of the things

00:06:33.473 --> 00:06:35.953
that's happened a lot since
we've been in the industry

00:06:35.953 --> 00:06:40.089
is we get a lot of people
calling the press anonymously.

00:06:40.089 --> 00:06:42.009
They are probably more
anonymous quotes (chuckles)

00:06:42.009 --> 00:06:45.412
about us in the press than
any other entity right now.

00:06:45.412 --> 00:06:47.381
And Sarah calls me up and she goes,

00:06:47.381 --> 00:06:50.443
Ben, I got a tip from six
different anonymous angels

00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:53.987
saying that you're doing
this and I'm going,

00:06:53.987 --> 00:06:55.640
if they don't like what I'm doing,

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.603
why don't they just come out and say it?

00:06:57.603 --> 00:06:58.701
(Sarah chuckles)
Like if I don't like something

00:06:58.701 --> 00:07:00.020
somebody's doing, I just come out.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:03.588
I put my name on it and
the reason that bothers me

00:07:03.588 --> 00:07:05.583
so much is the number
one thing we look for

00:07:05.583 --> 00:07:07.951
in an entrepreneur and the
number one thing that I encourage

00:07:07.951 --> 00:07:11.280
and I try and help people
develop is courage.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.752
And the reason that that's important

00:07:13.752 --> 00:07:17.056
and it's very, very important
and it goes back to Aristotle,

00:07:17.056 --> 00:07:20.707
so Aristotle said courage
is the first virtue,

00:07:20.707 --> 00:07:22.417
and the reason it's the first virtue

00:07:22.417 --> 00:07:25.486
is if you don't have it, then
you don't have any virtue

00:07:25.486 --> 00:07:28.940
because all these other
things, honesty, integrity,

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:32.521
whatever, don't ever get activated

00:07:32.521 --> 00:07:34.720
when they're important if
you don't have courage.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.150
It's easy to be honest unless
it's going to cost you your job

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.460
or your marriage or
something you care about

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.431
then it's hard to be honest.

00:07:42.431 --> 00:07:45.537
So without courage, you
don't have integrity.

00:07:45.537 --> 00:07:48.167
You don't have anything,
you're just like the,

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:51.893
you're a zero-character
person and so when people

00:07:51.893 --> 00:07:54.508
who are supposed to be
coaching entrepreneurs,

00:07:54.508 --> 00:07:58.087
angel investors or VCs walk around giving

00:07:58.087 --> 00:08:00.767
anonymous tips to reporters about stuff

00:08:00.767 --> 00:08:03.793
they don't think is right, and those guys

00:08:03.793 --> 00:08:05.911
are advising the people I'm working with?

00:08:05.911 --> 00:08:09.583
That really, that's
something that pissed me off

00:08:09.583 --> 00:08:12.630
so like I'm not having it
and I'm going to call 'em

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.880
on it (chuckles) and
they come at me again,

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.487
you anonymous quote me (chuckles).

00:08:16.487 --> 00:08:17.655
(audience laughing)

00:08:17.655 --> 00:08:18.778
- I can't wait to get--
- I kill you!

00:08:18.778 --> 00:08:20.028
- An anonymous quote tomorrow.

00:08:20.028 --> 00:08:23.152
- Did you imagine you'd be
managing almost five billion?

00:08:23.152 --> 00:08:26.039
- So one of the things we
knew from entrepreneurs

00:08:26.039 --> 00:08:29.896
and this is very, very key
point on entrepreneurship

00:08:29.896 --> 00:08:33.076
is that it is just as much
work and just as traumatic

00:08:33.076 --> 00:08:37.569
to build a company that's
trivial and nobody cares about

00:08:37.569 --> 00:08:40.356
than it is to build a really
significant important thing.

00:08:40.356 --> 00:08:42.821
It's the same amount of work,
so you might as well try to do

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:44.840
something important.
(audience chuckles)

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.345
And so that was always
our thought with the firm.

00:08:47.345 --> 00:08:49.796
We were like, we are going
to like, our goal is to be

00:08:49.796 --> 00:08:51.870
the important venture
capital firm in the world.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:55.025
Like I said, it's completely
megalomaniacal and arrogant

00:08:55.025 --> 00:08:57.840
and idiotic but that
like, if you don't have

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.751
that attitude as an
entrepreneur, you should not be

00:08:59.751 --> 00:09:02.527
an entrepreneur, so, yeah,
(chuckles) we were always

00:09:02.527 --> 00:09:04.591
going to be the biggest, we
were always going to be the best

00:09:04.591 --> 00:09:08.501
and that was the goal and
there was no reason to exist

00:09:08.501 --> 00:09:10.724
if we weren't going to do that.

00:09:10.724 --> 00:09:14.447
What I'd like to give is a
few unconventional graduation

00:09:14.447 --> 00:09:18.614
thoughts and I'm titling
them do not call your passion

00:09:19.769 --> 00:09:24.482
and the world is not going
to hell in a hand basket

00:09:24.482 --> 00:09:28.079
and the Class of 2015 is
not required to save it.

00:09:28.079 --> 00:09:30.796
(audience chuckles)

00:09:30.796 --> 00:09:33.405
I told you it was going
to be unconventional.

00:09:33.405 --> 00:09:36.003
So don't follow your passion.

00:09:36.003 --> 00:09:39.887
Now, you're probably thinking
that's a really dumb idea

00:09:39.887 --> 00:09:43.971
because everybody who's
successful and if you poll

00:09:43.971 --> 00:09:46.322
1,000 people who are
successful, they'll all say

00:09:46.322 --> 00:09:50.508
that they love what they do
and so the broad conclusion

00:09:50.508 --> 00:09:53.278
of that world is that
if you do what you love,

00:09:53.278 --> 00:09:56.639
then you'll be successful
but we're engineers

00:09:56.639 --> 00:09:58.973
and we know that that might be true.

00:09:58.973 --> 00:10:01.795
But it also might be the case
that if you're successful,

00:10:01.795 --> 00:10:04.776
you love what you do, you
just love being successful

00:10:04.776 --> 00:10:06.643
and everybody love you, it's awesome.

00:10:06.643 --> 00:10:10.810
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:10:13.855 --> 00:10:15.806
So which one is it?

00:10:15.806 --> 00:10:18.382
Well, I think to figure it out,
you have to go back in time.

00:10:18.382 --> 00:10:20.653
You have to back off
when you were successful

00:10:20.653 --> 00:10:24.373
to right now, when you're
graduating as the Class of 2015.

00:10:24.373 --> 00:10:27.235
And the first tricky thing about passions

00:10:27.235 --> 00:10:30.933
are they're hard to prioritize
which passion is it.

00:10:30.933 --> 00:10:33.566
Are you more passionate
about math or engineering?

00:10:33.566 --> 00:10:38.053
Are you more passionate
about history or literature?

00:10:38.053 --> 00:10:41.853
Are you more passionate
about video games or K-pop?

00:10:41.853 --> 00:10:43.161
(audience chuckling)

00:10:43.161 --> 00:10:46.824
These are tough decisions,
how do you even know?

00:10:46.824 --> 00:10:49.441
On the other hand, what are you good at?

00:10:49.441 --> 00:10:52.033
Are you good at math or writing?

00:10:52.033 --> 00:10:54.819
That's a much easier thing to figure out.

00:10:54.819 --> 00:10:57.093
The second thing that's
tricky if you're going forward

00:10:57.093 --> 00:10:59.947
in time with this follow-your-passion idea

00:10:59.947 --> 00:11:03.224
is that what you're passionate about at 21

00:11:03.224 --> 00:11:07.976
is not necessarily what you're
going to be passionate at 40.

00:11:07.976 --> 00:11:11.679
Now this is true for boyfriends
as well as career choices.

00:11:11.679 --> 00:11:14.862
(audience laughing)

00:11:14.862 --> 00:11:18.779
The third issue with
following your passion is,

00:11:20.808 --> 00:11:23.042
excuse me, a little
trouble with the throat,

00:11:23.042 --> 00:11:24.969
'cause you're not necessarily
good at your passion

00:11:24.969 --> 00:11:28.118
and has anybody ever watch American Idol?

00:11:28.118 --> 00:11:30.823
So you know what I'm talking
about, just because you love

00:11:30.823 --> 00:11:33.240
singing doesn't mean you should
be a professional singer.

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:34.936
(audience laughing)

00:11:34.936 --> 00:11:37.167
And then finally and most importantly,

00:11:37.167 --> 00:11:40.417
following your passion is
a very me-centered view

00:11:40.417 --> 00:11:43.960
of the world and when you go through life,

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.800
what you'll find is what you take

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:50.060
out of the world over
time, be it, whatever,

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.499
money, cars, stuff, accolades,

00:11:52.499 --> 00:11:56.030
is much less important than
what you put into the world.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:58.513
And so my recommendation would be follow

00:11:58.513 --> 00:12:01.839
your contribution, find the
thing that you're great at,

00:12:01.839 --> 00:12:04.892
put that into the world,
contribute to others,

00:12:04.892 --> 00:12:09.059
help the world be better and
that is the thing to follow.

00:12:10.544 --> 00:12:13.169
It's funny, sometimes I
talk to my wife about this

00:12:13.169 --> 00:12:16.141
but hard work is super important in life

00:12:16.141 --> 00:12:19.571
and now, I grew up in Berkeley,
I didn't have any money.

00:12:19.571 --> 00:12:21.910
I played football for the Yellow Jackets.

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:23.577
I was just like a kid in the neighborhood

00:12:23.577 --> 00:12:26.241
and now, I have a lot
money and so with my kids,

00:12:26.241 --> 00:12:28.373
I'm like, okay, now that I'm rich,

00:12:28.373 --> 00:12:30.320
how do I teach you the value of hard work?

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.152
'Cause that's really the
only important lesson

00:12:32.152 --> 00:12:36.542
that I've got, but for
all of you, I think most

00:12:36.542 --> 00:12:39.013
of you out of public
university probably know

00:12:39.013 --> 00:12:40.645
a little of the value of hard work.

00:12:40.645 --> 00:12:44.592
Keep that lesson, that
work is what's important.

00:12:44.592 --> 00:12:47.573
It's that effort where
you learn the secrets

00:12:47.573 --> 00:12:50.789
that are super valuable and can change

00:12:50.789 --> 00:12:53.172
people's lives and change the world.

00:12:53.172 --> 00:12:55.373
- But why did you still
believe in the company?

00:12:55.373 --> 00:12:59.337
I have to think that at any
point in Silicon Valley history,

00:12:59.337 --> 00:13:01.150
there's a lot of people
that at that point,

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:03.582
they're just like, this
isn't going to work.

00:13:03.582 --> 00:13:07.749
Market's changed, this isn't
a viable concept anymore.

00:13:14.061 --> 00:13:17.949
- I don't know how much
belief I had but I knew

00:13:17.949 --> 00:13:21.343
that I had gotten everybody
into the boat with me

00:13:21.343 --> 00:13:25.135
so it wasn't so much that
I didn't want to quit.

00:13:25.135 --> 00:13:27.519
I wanted to quit every day
(chuckles) I was in that job.

00:13:27.519 --> 00:13:30.045
I felt like throwing up when
I woke up in the morning.

00:13:30.045 --> 00:13:31.490
(audience laughing)

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:32.853
- (chuckles) So you know
what's it's like having

00:13:32.853 --> 00:13:34.234
a baby now (chuckles).
- Yeah, exactly.

00:13:34.234 --> 00:13:37.213
Like I felt like I was pregnant

00:13:37.213 --> 00:13:41.424
but I'd hired every employee
and they all trusted me.

00:13:41.424 --> 00:13:43.665
I'd raised all the money,
I talked to every investor

00:13:43.665 --> 00:13:47.016
into putting their money
in, I had given stock

00:13:47.016 --> 00:13:50.152
to my friends and relatives
and they were all excited

00:13:50.152 --> 00:13:52.154
about the prospects for the company.

00:13:52.154 --> 00:13:54.229
When it went public
like a lot of my friends

00:13:54.229 --> 00:13:56.425
from high school bought the
stock 'cause I was running

00:13:56.425 --> 00:14:00.008
the company, and so I
felt like I could die

00:14:01.286 --> 00:14:04.217
but I couldn't quit and one--

00:14:04.217 --> 00:14:05.821
- Wow.
- Of the horrible things

00:14:05.821 --> 00:14:07.904
about being founder
CEO is it's the one job

00:14:07.904 --> 00:14:10.842
you really can't quit or if you do quit,

00:14:10.842 --> 00:14:13.790
you're a punk.
(Sarah and audience laughing)

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:16.528
And so I didn't want to be a punk

00:14:16.528 --> 00:14:18.528
so I just kept peddling.

00:14:21.359 --> 00:14:24.745
But it was rough, I mean
that was, running LoudCloud

00:14:24.745 --> 00:14:26.876
(chuckles)
Right? And it didn't stop

00:14:26.876 --> 00:14:29.655
there, right, like that
was LoudCloud (chuckles).

00:14:29.655 --> 00:14:34.003
LoudCloud, by the time
LoudCloud was 2 1/2 years old,

00:14:34.003 --> 00:14:38.475
I had sold the LoudCloud
part of the business

00:14:38.475 --> 00:14:40.742
and I had to change it into
another business (chuckles)

00:14:40.742 --> 00:14:43.625
so the next thing that
happened was LoudCloud

00:14:43.625 --> 00:14:46.429
was going to go bankrupt,
so I raised $160 million

00:14:46.429 --> 00:14:50.063
in the IPO but we burned through that

00:14:50.063 --> 00:14:52.044
and we couldn't stop the
burn because the customers

00:14:52.044 --> 00:14:56.119
kept going out of business
and so we were headed

00:14:56.119 --> 00:14:59.304
towards bankruptcy so then I had to,

00:14:59.304 --> 00:15:02.406
I sold (chuckles) the
services part of the business

00:15:02.406 --> 00:15:04.681
and all the cash part
into EDS and then I had

00:15:04.681 --> 00:15:09.005
to lay off probably another
third of the employees

00:15:09.005 --> 00:15:11.962
and then I kept the remaining 80 employees

00:15:11.962 --> 00:15:13.939
and we became a software company,

00:15:13.939 --> 00:15:16.537
a publicly trade software
company with no customers

00:15:16.537 --> 00:15:19.538
and no revenue, which was another trick--

00:15:19.538 --> 00:15:20.861
- That was when you became Opsware.

00:15:20.861 --> 00:15:24.655
- That's when we became
Opsware, yeah, so that was hard.

00:15:24.655 --> 00:15:26.611
(Sarah and audience chuckling)

00:15:26.611 --> 00:15:28.587
But it was good, it
had that kind of stuff.

00:15:28.587 --> 00:15:31.242
It makes you strong.
(Sarah and audience chuckling)

00:15:31.242 --> 00:15:34.413
Eventually, five years later,
we sold to Hewlett Packard

00:15:34.413 --> 00:15:35.830
for $1.6 billion.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:39.269
- Not a bad exit.

00:15:39.269 --> 00:15:42.563
- Which was still, I'm still
shocked by that whole thing

00:15:42.563 --> 00:15:43.684
(Sarah and Ben chuckling)
I still wake up

00:15:43.684 --> 00:15:45.612
in the middle of the night going,
are we going to run of money?

00:15:45.612 --> 00:15:48.262
You only get leverage,
and this is something

00:15:48.262 --> 00:15:51.895
that is really tricky for
founding CEOs to get to,

00:15:51.895 --> 00:15:56.061
but you only leverage if
the person that you hire

00:15:56.061 --> 00:16:00.460
can do it better than you can
because as long as you feel

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.373
like you're better than them at it,

00:16:02.373 --> 00:16:04.758
then there's not, you're just
going to keep second-guessing

00:16:04.758 --> 00:16:07.094
them and you're not going
to get any leverage.

00:16:07.094 --> 00:16:08.972
And a lot of the times,
I'll be talking to CEOs

00:16:08.972 --> 00:16:11.186
and they'll have an executive
and they'll be like,

00:16:11.186 --> 00:16:13.222
oh, this person's really
good and they're working

00:16:13.222 --> 00:16:16.429
really hard and I'm like, well,
but you're always frustrated

00:16:16.429 --> 00:16:20.233
about, you always feel like
you have to do that yourself

00:16:20.233 --> 00:16:21.973
and it's like, yeah, 'cause
they don't understand

00:16:21.973 --> 00:16:23.756
how to do this and that, and I'm like,

00:16:23.756 --> 00:16:26.611
well, then they're not an executive

00:16:26.611 --> 00:16:28.477
because the whole point of an executive

00:16:28.477 --> 00:16:32.552
is to get you leverage and
so on a question like that,

00:16:32.552 --> 00:16:35.758
it's like okay, where can
you find the right person

00:16:35.758 --> 00:16:38.861
and make the hire to get you
leverage, and wherever that is,

00:16:38.861 --> 00:16:41.315
that's what you should give up first.

00:16:41.315 --> 00:16:45.482
And you want leverage on
all spots because as CEO,

00:16:46.336 --> 00:16:49.047
you can be the keeper of the vision,

00:16:49.047 --> 00:16:52.409
the quality control over
the top but you can't be

00:16:52.409 --> 00:16:55.693
the lead engineer forever, there is no,

00:16:55.693 --> 00:16:57.649
and it doesn't matter
how good an engineer,

00:16:57.649 --> 00:17:00.124
like Larry Page, as great
an engineer as he was,

00:17:00.124 --> 00:17:03.022
is not an engineer at
Google, he just isn't.

00:17:03.022 --> 00:17:06.103
And he cannot work on
production products (chuckles).

00:17:06.103 --> 00:17:08.411
It's a full-time job, even
for somebody who can do

00:17:08.411 --> 00:17:11.208
as many things as Larry, he cannot do that

00:17:11.208 --> 00:17:13.286
and nobody can, like if Larry can't do it,

00:17:13.286 --> 00:17:15.118
you can't do it either, I guarantee you.

00:17:15.118 --> 00:17:16.534
(audience laughing)

00:17:16.534 --> 00:17:18.753
And so you're going to
have to give them all up

00:17:18.753 --> 00:17:21.829
and so it's just a matter of
where do you get the leverage.

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:23.605
All of the general partners in the firm

00:17:23.605 --> 00:17:26.554
basically agreed that we would
give half of our money away

00:17:26.554 --> 00:17:30.029
to charity that we earned at the firm,

00:17:30.029 --> 00:17:34.025
basically half of everything we
get, we're going to give away.

00:17:34.025 --> 00:17:38.192
And here's why, here's why,
let me just say why we did it.

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:41.017
The world is complex

00:17:46.842 --> 00:17:49.259
and it can't be entirely fair

00:17:51.304 --> 00:17:54.303
from what you contribute to what you get

00:17:54.303 --> 00:17:57.113
is very, very uneven and
even if you just look

00:17:57.113 --> 00:18:00.059
at Silicon Valley, if you look

00:18:00.059 --> 00:18:03.659
at the kind of brilliant
people at Stanford Hospital

00:18:03.659 --> 00:18:07.653
or some of the people
who build the buildings

00:18:07.653 --> 00:18:10.609
that we live in or the
guys who teach the kids

00:18:10.609 --> 00:18:14.583
at the teachers at the
schools are incredibly smart

00:18:14.583 --> 00:18:18.536
and dedicated but there really is no way

00:18:18.536 --> 00:18:22.691
that they're going to ever
make anything like what I make

00:18:22.691 --> 00:18:25.249
at Andreessen Horowitz,
it's just impossible

00:18:25.249 --> 00:18:26.893
the way society is structured.

00:18:26.893 --> 00:18:31.060
There's no way it could
happen but their contribution

00:18:32.791 --> 00:18:35.705
to what I do is significant, without them,

00:18:35.705 --> 00:18:38.238
I've got kids that are in those schools.

00:18:38.238 --> 00:18:40.266
I get sick, I go to those doctors.

00:18:40.266 --> 00:18:44.183
I live in that environment
and I appreciate it.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:48.149
I really appreciate
the effort and the work

00:18:48.149 --> 00:18:52.709
and what they do and so
for me to be in a position

00:18:52.709 --> 00:18:56.502
where I can put money into
the resources that I share

00:18:56.502 --> 00:18:59.305
with them and make their
life a little easier,

00:18:59.305 --> 00:19:01.405
I just feel like it's
the right thing to do

00:19:01.405 --> 00:19:03.552
so it's nothing more than
that and I don't there's

00:19:03.552 --> 00:19:06.829
any kind of way I can give
money to other people.

00:19:06.829 --> 00:19:10.675
If you're a technology
entrepreneur, you've got

00:19:10.675 --> 00:19:13.152
every opportunity to make
like a gazillion dollars.

00:19:13.152 --> 00:19:16.467
That opportunity is
there whether they invest

00:19:16.467 --> 00:19:18.851
and get a percentage
of your company or not.

00:19:18.851 --> 00:19:22.095
But if you're a teacher, you
don't have that opportunity

00:19:22.095 --> 00:19:25.146
but, man, if I can get you
the resources to do your job

00:19:25.146 --> 00:19:27.237
better so that you've
got a little more space

00:19:27.237 --> 00:19:30.001
in your classroom, that
could make a difference

00:19:30.001 --> 00:19:32.205
for a kid who can do something
that's really important

00:19:32.205 --> 00:19:34.563
and so that's what I'm
about and that's what

00:19:34.563 --> 00:19:37.228
I'm going to do and I'm very
proud that our whole firm

00:19:37.228 --> 00:19:39.804
is doing that with me.

00:19:39.804 --> 00:19:42.381
- The Hard Thing About
Hard Things, which is just,

00:19:42.381 --> 00:19:44.634
where did that title come from?

00:19:44.634 --> 00:19:46.140
(chuckles)
'Cause that's just

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:48.816
a way cool title, The Hard
Thing About Hard Things.

00:19:48.816 --> 00:19:50.540
- Well, actually, I
wrote that title myself,

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:52.394
which I understand is actually pretty rare

00:19:52.394 --> 00:19:55.241
in the book business that they
let you keep your own title.

00:19:55.241 --> 00:19:59.074
But where it came from
was when I was the CEO,

00:20:01.284 --> 00:20:04.627
it was easy time, I slept like a baby.

00:20:04.627 --> 00:20:07.114
I woke up every two hours and cried.

00:20:07.114 --> 00:20:09.207
(audience laughing)

00:20:09.207 --> 00:20:13.202
- (claps) I need a drum
roll (faint speaking).

00:20:13.202 --> 00:20:15.748
- And you know what, when I would be up

00:20:15.748 --> 00:20:18.236
and (chuckles) this is not a
joke, this is actually true.

00:20:18.236 --> 00:20:19.924
You can ask my wife, I'd
be up at three o'clock

00:20:19.924 --> 00:20:23.523
in the morning just sitting
there going why are none

00:20:23.523 --> 00:20:25.798
of the management books
that I'm reading helping me?

00:20:25.798 --> 00:20:29.465
They are no help at all
and I read literally

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:34.527
every management book and I
realized that management books

00:20:35.743 --> 00:20:39.275
are generally written for here's how you,

00:20:39.275 --> 00:20:42.731
to not screw up your company
but if you start a company,

00:20:42.731 --> 00:20:46.062
that lasts like a week and
then you've screwed it up

00:20:46.062 --> 00:20:48.308
and then where's the book for that?

00:20:48.308 --> 00:20:49.473
(audience chuckling)

00:20:49.473 --> 00:20:51.316
And that's what was missing
so I would think about it.

00:20:51.316 --> 00:20:54.602
I'd be like okay, yeah, so
all the VCs and everybody

00:20:54.602 --> 00:20:59.493
tells me, Ben, you know what
the key to a great company is?

00:20:59.493 --> 00:21:02.029
Hire A players.
(audience chuckles)

00:21:02.029 --> 00:21:03.620
And I'd be sitting
there like, okay, great,

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:04.801
'cause I was going to hire a bunch

00:21:04.801 --> 00:21:06.599
of (bleep) idiots.
(audience laughing)

00:21:06.599 --> 00:21:10.849
And now you've unlocked the
secret for me, thank you.

00:21:10.849 --> 00:21:14.985
But that wasn't like way and
I was like the hard thing

00:21:14.985 --> 00:21:17.343
is not to hire the best people, right?

00:21:17.343 --> 00:21:21.088
The hard thing is when your
company is going like this

00:21:21.088 --> 00:21:23.348
and the great, best people
don't want to work for you

00:21:23.348 --> 00:21:26.881
and then who do you hire
and how does that work?

00:21:26.881 --> 00:21:30.504
Do you hire the person
who's okay across the board

00:21:30.504 --> 00:21:32.108
or do you hire the person
that's got something

00:21:32.108 --> 00:21:35.008
horribly wrong with them
but super great about them

00:21:35.008 --> 00:21:37.332
and how do you think about
that and what do you do

00:21:37.332 --> 00:21:40.748
with the horribly wrong
stuff once you get them?

00:21:40.748 --> 00:21:44.361
That's the hard thing and
there's I read the Jim Collins

00:21:44.361 --> 00:21:48.039
books, which I hate.
(audience laughing)

00:21:48.039 --> 00:21:50.359
(chuckles) Some of you like them, I know.

00:21:50.359 --> 00:21:52.333
It doesn't mean you're
not smart if you like them

00:21:52.333 --> 00:21:54.062
because a lot of smart people like them

00:21:54.062 --> 00:21:56.671
but the thing that he does
is he makes you feel good

00:21:56.671 --> 00:21:59.529
about yourself but then
when you get into it,

00:21:59.529 --> 00:22:03.036
you realize cause and
effect is all swung around

00:22:03.036 --> 00:22:04.685
in all these things but he's like, big,

00:22:04.685 --> 00:22:07.672
what great companies have is
a big hairy audacious goal

00:22:07.672 --> 00:22:09.579
and I think that's great
but that's no hard.

00:22:09.579 --> 00:22:11.462
It's pretty easy to write a big hairy,

00:22:11.462 --> 00:22:13.284
like we're going to go
to the moon, you know?

00:22:13.284 --> 00:22:14.808
We're going to build the
biggest company in the world.

00:22:14.808 --> 00:22:16.312
We're going to have
software on every desktop.

00:22:16.312 --> 00:22:18.198
It's all going to be ours,
like that's our goal.

00:22:18.198 --> 00:22:20.771
Okay, so now you've missed your goal.

00:22:20.771 --> 00:22:22.000
(audience chuckling)
And not only did you miss

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.652
your goal but you built
up your whole company

00:22:23.652 --> 00:22:25.284
to achieve your goal and
you have a cost structure

00:22:25.284 --> 00:22:27.484
that's designed to hit your
goal and you've missed your goal

00:22:27.484 --> 00:22:29.890
and you're running out of
cash and the company's burning

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:32.476
to the ground and all the
company, everybody that works

00:22:32.476 --> 00:22:35.099
for you thinks you're a moron
because you missed your goal.

00:22:35.099 --> 00:22:37.659
Okay, that's hard (chuckles)
like that's a hard thing.

00:22:37.659 --> 00:22:40.078
What do you do there, well,
that's not in Jim's book, he

00:22:40.078 --> 00:22:41.536
didn't even talk about that.
(audience laughing)

00:22:41.536 --> 00:22:44.117
It's like nobody ever misses
our goal who's a great company

00:22:44.117 --> 00:22:45.958
so why would you miss your goal?

00:22:45.958 --> 00:22:48.134
You must not be great.
(audience laughing)

00:22:48.134 --> 00:22:50.841
(laughter drowns out voice) Jim so anyway,

00:22:50.841 --> 00:22:52.968
that's where The Hard
Thing About Hard Things.

00:22:52.968 --> 00:22:53.932
(audience clapping)

00:22:53.932 --> 00:22:55.002
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:22:55.002 --> 00:22:59.004
I made this video because
FBGM 247 asked me to.

00:22:59.004 --> 00:23:01.471
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:23:01.471 --> 00:23:03.379
to profile next, leave it in the comments

00:23:03.379 --> 00:23:05.031
below and I'll see what I can do.

00:23:05.031 --> 00:23:07.948
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:23:07.948 --> 00:23:10.705
is going to have the
biggest impact on your life.

00:23:10.705 --> 00:23:12.737
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join the discussion.

00:23:12.737 --> 00:23:14.964
And if you liked this
video, my personal goal

00:23:14.964 --> 00:23:17.958
on this channel is to help
reach a million subscribers

00:23:17.958 --> 00:23:20.210
so anything you can think
of to help share the videos,

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:23.203
spread the message, I really,
truly, deeply appreciate it.

00:23:23.203 --> 00:23:24.986
Thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:23:24.986 --> 00:23:27.935
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:23:27.935 --> 00:23:30.353
(zapping)
(thunder rumbling)

